# sanitize.sh substitution table — copy to sanitize.local.tsv and fill in the
# values your appliance actually returns. That file is gitignored: it names
# real identifiers, which is exactly what must not be committed to a public
# repo.
#
# Format: value<TAB>replacement, one per line. "#" comments and blank lines
# are ignored. Matching is literal (no regex), applied across both
# mock-semp/canned/ and fidelity/golden/ so the two sides stay consistent —
# an inconsistent substitution fails the exact-mode fidelity gate.
#
# Find the values to list by running ./sanitize.sh once: its residual scan
# names every MAC, lab IP, WWPN, and non-GA build marker still present.
# Serials it cannot find — those you read out of show_hardware_details.xml
# yourself (chassis, board, disk, blade, flash, SFP).
#
# Placeholder conventions, so fixtures stay readable and obviously synthetic:
#
#   TESTSERIAL-<PART>-NN   serials. Keep the original field width where the
#                          wire format is space-padded (SFP serials are
#                          16 chars including trailing spaces).
#   00:00:5E:00:53:xx      MACs. RFC 7042 documentation range.
#   198.51.100.x           IPs. TEST-NET-2, RFC 5737.
#   0x2100000000000001     WWPN / 0x2000000000000001 WWNN. Keep the low-nibble
#     pairing of the originals: real FC addressing shares it, and preserving
#     that keeps the fixture recognizable to someone who knows the format.
#   10.25.1.10             build strings. Any plausible GA form; must not
#                          carry a "+lo.NNN" or "NNNmain.N" marker, which the
#                          residual scan rejects.
#
# Example shape (values here are already synthetic — replace them):

S000000000	TESTSERIAL-CHASSIS-01
AA00000000	TESTSERIAL-BOARD-01
192.168.0.10	198.51.100.10
aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff	00:00:5E:00:53:01
0x2100000000000000	0x2100000000000001
100.0main.0.0000	10.25.1.10
